    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Vertical relay zones form in shallow-marine carbonates where normal fault segments overlap across brittle beds. In many reservoirs and aquifers in central and eastern Texas, it is believed that these relay zones localize fluid flow since the rocks within them are often highly strained and exhibit different properties from the surrounding formations. Vertical relay zones in the Cretaceous Austin Chalk formation in Ellis, Co, Texas have intricate internal architectures which may be partitioned into five different constituents. The flow of fluids through these relay zones is likely to be controlled by the thicknesses of three of these constituents: calcite filled veins, open fractures, and brecciated zones. Field measurements of these constituents demonstrate that the thicknesses of these constituents evolve as the throw on the fault segements increases, and that their trends may be represented with simple equations. These empirical relationships serve as a basis to estimate the effective permeability of vertical relay zones as a function of fault throw.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
